SRE and Manager of Managers Operating Model

Roles, responsibilities, shared SRE practices, and service onboarding
Date: 7 October 2026
Status: Proposed organizational operating model

1. Purpose and scope

Establish a practical SRE operating model across business lines, service owners, SRE, application support, development or vendors, infrastructure teams, monitoring engineering, and command-center operators.

The model assigns accountability for service outcomes, execution of reliability practices, operation of the monitoring environment, incident response, and acceptance of services into centralized monitoring.

The confirmed operating arrangement is that command-center operators watch the monitoring screens. This document proposes that monitoring engineering builds and maintains the monitoring capability. That engineering responsibility can be assigned to an existing monitoring or platform team.

Manager of managers (MoM) means the technology layer that consolidates alerts and service information from multiple monitoring tools. The actual product has not been specified. Splunk IT Service Intelligence (ITSI) is used only to illustrate documented capabilities; product-specific configuration must be adapted to the deployed platform and version.

The organizational assignments, acceptance gates, and governance arrangements below are recommendations for this organization. Google SRE, ITIL, and Splunk documentation provide the underlying practices and capabilities; they do not prescribe this exact organizational chart.

2. Operating principles

1. One accountable service owner: each business service has a named owner with authority to coordinate priorities, funding, and risk decisions through established governance.
2. Shared execution with explicit ownership: each activity has a lead, named contributors, and a measurable completion criterion.
3. Business outcomes guide reliability: measure the user journeys that matter, such as completing a payment or receiving a timely inquiry response.
4. Command center operates the monitoring view: operators detect, acknowledge, qualify, route, and track operational events.
5. Monitoring engineering maintains the capability: engineers maintain integrations, service models, dashboards, rules, routing, and telemetry quality.
6. Technical teams own their changes and recovery: application, infrastructure, database, network, development, and vendor teams retain responsibility for their components.
7. SRE has an explicit engagement scope: specify supported services, engineering obligations, incident triggers, access, and coverage commitments.
8. Service acceptance is evidence-based: require tested detection, routing, recovery, and ownership before accepting operational obligations.
9. Protect engineering capacity: measure operational workload and reserve capacity for improvements that reduce recurring failures and manual work.

Google’s engagement guidance supports agreeing service scope, shared goals, responsibilities, and sustainable levels of SRE involvement. 1, 2

3. Roles and responsibilities

|Team or role                                            |Actual responsibilities                                                                                                                                                                                         |Principal deliverables and decisions                                                                                      |
|--------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**Business line**                                       |Identify critical journeys, business operating periods, impact of disruption, acceptable service performance, and future demand. Validate business recovery.                                                    |Business requirements, criticality, demand assumptions, and business validation criteria.                                 |
|**Named service owner**                                 |Remain accountable for the end-to-end service. Agree objectives with business and technical teams, prioritize investment, resolve ownership conflicts, and obtain required risk acceptance.                     |Approved service objectives, prioritized improvement backlog, service acceptance, and documented risk decisions.          |
|**SRE**                                                 |Design reliability measurement; analyze error budgets and systemic weaknesses; engineer automation; assess capacity and resilience; review readiness; implement assigned improvements; support agreed incidents.|SLI/SLO specifications, reliability reports, tested automation, engineering improvements, and readiness findings.         |
