SLI/SLO calculations can run in a source monitoring platform, a dedicated reliability service, or the MoM where its capabilities support the agreed calculation. Maintain one authoritative definition and link the operational views to it.

5.2 Required MoM capabilities and ownership

|Capability                       |Operational use                                                                                    |Proposed owner                                                                                                                 |
|---------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**Business-service model**       |Connect business services to applications, relevant components, and dependencies.                  |Monitoring engineering maintains configuration; application/infrastructure teams supply topology; service owner approves scope.|
|**Event normalization**          |Map source alerts into consistent identity, severity, time, entity, and lifecycle fields.          |Monitoring engineering with source-tool owners.                                                                                |
|**Correlation and deduplication**|Group related symptoms and reduce repeated work while retaining original evidence.                 |Monitoring engineering; resolver teams validate the grouping.                                                                  |
|**Service-health views**         |Show affected journeys, service status, dependencies, and uncertainty caused by missing data.      |Monitoring engineering maintains; command center defines operational usability needs.                                          |
|**Priority and routing**         |Combine impact, urgency, service criticality, resolver ownership, and support coverage.            |Service owner agrees business priority; monitoring configures; command center applies and escalates.                           |
|**Reliability views**            |Display SLO results and error-budget information or link to the authoritative reports.             |SRE owns measurement design for engaged services; monitoring implements presentation/integration.                              |
|**ITSM integration**             |Create/update incidents and preserve links among source events, MoM episodes, and incident records.|Monitoring/platform integration owner; ITSM owner defines record and lifecycle rules.                                          |
|**Approved operational actions** |Invoke controlled diagnostics or recovery actions with recorded results.                           |Automation owner maintains the action; relevant authority defines execution permissions.                                       |
|**Platform health**              |Detect ingestion lag, connector failure, stale dashboards, processing errors, and platform outage. |Named MoM platform owner.                                                                                                      |

Splunk ITSI documents service-health visualization, third-party alert ingestion, event normalization, episode workflows, and aggregation policies. These are product capabilities that require configuration and operating ownership. 9, 10, 11, 12

5.3 Distinguish health scores, service levels, and incidents

|Record or measure                       |Meaning                                                                                                      |
|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**Service-health score**                |A summary of configured KPI/component states and their weighting.                                            |
|**SLI**                                 |A measurement of an agreed service outcome, such as successful transactions divided by eligible transactions.|
|**SLO**                                 |A target for the SLI over an agreed measurement window.                                                      |
|**Error budget**                        |The amount of failure allowed by the SLO over that window.                                                   |
|**MoM episode / correlated event group**|Operationally related events grouped for investigation.                                                      |
|**ITSM incident**                       |The authoritative restoration record in this proposed operating model.                                       |

An ITSI health score is not automatically SLO attainment. Alert counts alone cannot establish a transaction-success SLI because they do not provide the full population of eligible transactions. 4, 13

Detailed logs and traces can remain in existing tools, with drill-down links from the MoM. Choose the data required for operational visibility and measurement rather than duplicating every source dataset.

Treat missing or stale telemetry as a distinct condition. Provide an independent notification route for MoM failure. Correlation suggests relationships for investigation; technical teams validate causes.

6. Service onboarding process

Onboarding is complete when the organization can detect, assign, investigate, recover, and verify a service issue using tested procedures.

|Step                                       |Lead and contributors                                                          |Required completion evidence                                                                                                                                |
|-------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**1. Register the service**                |Service owner, business, application support                                   |Service ID, business purpose, critical journeys, tier, operating hours, accountable owner, support groups, and vendor contacts.                             |
|**2. Map dependencies**                    |Application support, development/vendor, infrastructure; monitoring configures |Relevant application components, database, network, messaging, identity, and external dependencies; maintained entity identifiers.                          |
|**3. Define service health and indicators**|SRE with business and technical owners                                         |User-success and latency measures; correctness, freshness, or batch-completion measures where relevant; supporting component signals; missing-data behavior.|
|**4. Connect monitoring sources**          |Monitoring engineering and source-tool owners                                  |Tested access, connectors, event delivery, measurement collection, timestamps, freshness, and diagnostic links.                                             |
|**5. Normalize and correlate**             |Monitoring engineering with resolver teams                                     |Common event structure, identity, severity mapping, open/update/clear handling, deduplication, and validated correlation.                                   |
|**6. Configure response**                  |Command center, monitoring, resolver teams, ITSM owner                         |Priority, assignment group, coverage, runbook, escalation path, ticket lifecycle, and maintenance handling.                                                 |
|**7. Test end to end**                     |Monitoring, command center, application/infrastructure teams; SRE where engaged|Controlled fault produces the correct signal, reaches an available responder, creates/routes the incident, and supports validated recovery.                 |
|**8. Accept service operations**           |Service owner; each operating team accepts its obligations                     |Acceptance record, residual risks, outstanding actions, named maintainers, and review triggers.                                                             |

The integration and normalization steps align with Splunk’s documented third-party event-ingestion and event-analytics guidance. The role assignments and acceptance requirements are proposed organizational controls. 10, 14

6.1 Minimum event and service context

Define a common information contract, adapting field names to the deployed MoM. Some values may be added through service-catalog enrichment rather than supplied by the original alert.

|Information                                                |Purpose                                                        |
|-----------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
|Source system and source event ID                          |Traceability, updates, and duplicate handling.                 |
|Service ID, environment, entity/component ID               |Associate the event with the correct service and dependency.   |
|Event type and summary                                     |Describe the condition and affected function.                  |
|Original severity and normalized severity                  |Preserve source meaning while supporting consistent operations.|
|State: open, updated, recovered/cleared                    |Maintain the event lifecycle correctly.                        |
|Occurrence, update, and ingestion timestamps               |Evaluate event age, latency, and ordering.                     |
|Stable deduplication key and correlation attributes        |Control repeated events and related-event grouping.            |
|Resolver group, coverage, and escalation contact           |Route work to an available owner.                              |
|Runbook and diagnostic links                               |Support repeatable investigation and first response.           |
|Related change, maintenance, or incident ID where available|Provide operational context and link records.                  |

Test retry behavior and duplicate handling. Define how late clear events, reopened failures, stale source state, and connector recovery affect the operational record.

6.2 Required acceptance tests

☐ A representative service failure produces the expected alert and service association.
☐ Performance degradation is visible through an appropriate user-impact signal.
☐ Duplicate events do not create uncontrolled duplicate incidents.
☐ Delayed and out-of-order events are handled according to the agreed lifecycle.
☐ Recovery/clear events update the operational view correctly.
☐ Missing telemetry and connector failures are visible and assigned.
☐ Maintenance suppression has a defined scope and expiry; detection resumes afterward.
☐ An unavailable primary responder triggers the agreed escalation.
☐ Correlation preserves visibility of separate failures and affected services.
☐ Diagnostic links, operator access, and runbooks work for the actual support roles.
☐ Functional recovery is validated using representative transactions or processing outcomes.
☐ Command-center operators demonstrate the procedure and accept the handover.

Service targets, alert thresholds, response objectives, and observation periods must be agreed for the service tier. This document does not impose generic recovery-time commitments.

7. Two separate acceptance gates

|Gate                                           |What it establishes                                                                      |Acceptance evidence                                                                                                                                                                |
|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**MoM / command-center operational acceptance**|The service can be monitored, routed, and supported through the agreed operational model.|Tested monitoring, event lifecycle, routing, responder coverage, runbooks, diagnostic access, and recovery validation.                                                             |
|**SRE engagement acceptance**                  |SRE has a defined and sustainable responsibility for improving or supporting the service.|Agreed scope, objectives, usable data, engineering access, participating technical/vendor owners, improvement priorities, staffing, and explicit on-call obligations if applicable.|

A service may receive advisory SRE support, a defined improvement engagement, or continuing production involvement. Select the engagement according to criticality, engineering opportunity, and available capacity. 1, 8

For vendor-supported applications, document access and modification limits. Use supported metrics, logs, diagnostic interfaces, and synthetic transactions where appropriate; assign product-code corrections to the vendor or application engineering team.

8. Incident and improvement workflow

1. Detect and qualify: command-center operators review the signal and available business impact. Monitoring engineering investigates signal or integration failures.
2. Assign and acknowledge: route to the appropriate resolver. Escalate unaccepted assignments under the coverage agreement.
3. Activate major-incident coordination when required: appoint incident command, communications, and technical leadership.
4. Diagnose and restore: the affected technical teams investigate and execute approved actions. SRE joins under agreed triggers, such as complex cross-service degradation or failure of SRE-owned automation.
5. Authorize exceptional actions: the relevant change or continuity authority approves actions requiring that authority.
6. Validate recovery: application support validates functional outcomes; command center verifies service indicators; business/service owner confirms recovery where required by the service procedure.
7. Close operational records: follow the agreed incident and episode lifecycle, preserving evidence and links.
8. Learn and improve: the problem owner coordinates review; technical owners implement actions; SRE examines systemic weaknesses; monitoring engineering updates signals and correlation.
9. Verify effectiveness: confirm that the improvement reduced recurrence, impact, risk, or operational effort before closing the action.

Google’s incident and postmortem guidance supports explicit response roles and learning that leads to tracked corrective work. 3, 7

9. Governance and measures of success

9.1 Service reliability review

The service owner leads the review with business, SRE, application support, monitoring, infrastructure, and development/vendor representatives as relevant.

Review:

• SLO results, business impact, and error-budget consumption.
• Repeated incidents and effectiveness of completed actions.
• Monitoring coverage, missed detections, noisy alerts, and telemetry gaps.
• Capacity, recovery, dependency, and upcoming-change risks.
• Manual operational effort and automation performance.
• Vendor issues, funding needs, and ownership disputes.

Each decision records one owner, priority, completion criterion, required evidence, and an agreed review point. The review cadence should match service criticality and operational needs.

9.2 Team measures

|Team / role                 |Recommended measures                                                                                                                                                                |
|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**Business / service owner**|User-impact duration, failed/delayed business transactions, agreed SLO results, and unresolved service risks.                                                                       |
|**Command center**          |Detection-to-acknowledgement and accepted-routing times, escalation adherence, assignment completeness, and communication quality.                                                  |
|**Monitoring engineering**  |Tested service coverage, telemetry freshness, missed detections, actionable-alert ratio, connector reliability, and correct event lifecycle handling.                               |
|**Application support**     |Validated restoration time, recurring application incidents, runbook success, vendor-case aging, and application change failures.                                                   |
|**Development / vendors**   |Defect recurrence, delivery and effectiveness of fixes, diagnostic capability, and implementation of agreed reliability requirements.                                               |
|**Infrastructure teams**    |Component reliability, capacity risk, change failures, restore/failover test results, and recurring infrastructure incidents.                                                       |
|**SRE**                     |SLO attainment for engaged services, verified impact/recurrence reduction, sustained automation benefit, engineering capacity, and completion of effective reliability improvements.|

Define measurement clocks and populations consistently. For example, restoration time ends at validated recovery, and its starting point should be the evidenced onset of impact where known. Separate results by service and severity; disclose unknown timestamps.

For automation, evaluate human effort saved after accounting for maintenance, failed executions, and rework. Google recommends measuring toil and automation effectiveness; its 50% operational-work cap is an example of protecting SRE engineering capacity, not a universal staffing requirement. 2

